Key domain knowledge: CycleNet (Linyi Li et al., "CycleNet: Enhancing Time Series Forecasting through Modeling Long-term Seasonalities", ICML 2024, openstic/CycleNet on GitHub). CycleNet uses a simple MLP plus a learnable cycle parameter (cycle length L) added as a cyclic index embedding; the key hyperparameter is the cycle length (e.g., 24 for hourly series with daily periodicity, 168 for weekly), and a cyclic index [1..L] repeated, embedded into a learnable parameter array of length L. It relies on partial series (last observation minus corresponding value one cycle earlier) — "partial series" = X - X shifted by L — to represent long-term seasonality. So the cycle length hyperparameter is central and set manually per dataset in the paper; a poor choice hurts. Autocorrelation analysis is a natural data-driven way: ACF peaks at lag = dominant seasonality; FFT periodogram peak; seasonal strength; also ACF after detrending/deseasonalizing. Related work: automatic seasonality detection (Atlas et al. "Sequencepedia", automatic feature extraction; "automatic time series feature extraction" — ACF-based dominant frequency, Hyndman's forecasts::nsdiffs / stl decomposition; FFT-based; ACF peak with harmonics suppression — e.g., dominant frequency = argmax of ACF restricted to integer multiples of fundamental, avoiding harmonic peaks at 2P, 3P).

Also note: CycleNet's L must satisfy that forecast horizon interacts: paper suggests L ≥ horizon? Actually CycleNet's cyclic parameter needs L and typically L larger than horizon; there's the constraint that cycle length should cover the horizon so that cyclic index covers the forecast region. I recall in CycleNet the cycle length is chosen e.g. 24 for ETT datasets... For traffic/electricity, period 24; weather 24; exchange 7. And L should ideally be a multiple of the intra-day sampling. I'll be careful and mark as needing verification from the repo (which is absent) — flag as an assumption to verify.

Methodological skeleton (10-15 lines) as requested. Include: ACF, PACF, normalized seasonal strength (e.g., Hyndman seasonal strength = max(0, 1 - var(resid)/var(detrend+resid))), periodogram/FFT cross-check, wavelet? keep light. Representations: raw log-transformed series, STL/differenced seasonal-differenced residual, detrended (moving-average), and ACF on differenced series to test whether peak is spurious trend-induced. Scoring: combine ACF peak height at candidate lag, spectral peak prominence, seasonal strength, and stability across rolling windows/series; harmonic suppression (candidate score = peak prominence relative to harmonics multiples), penalty for L outside feasible band [2, N/4] and L≥horizon; aggregate by median-of-favorites or weighted rank, choose single recommended L per series, then per-dataset majority vote.

Correlation-only vs model-needed: L recommendation is correlation-only; but validating that correlation-optimal L minimizes forecast error requires training CycleNet (or a cheap linear surrogate, e.g. ridge regression / seasonal-naive baseline) — that's the ground truth part. Also note a cheap proxy: leave-last-out held-out ACF-based fit of seasonal-naive with cycle L, i.e., seasonal-naive-with-cycle-L MSE — a model-free-ish check that is nearly free.

Open questions list (a)-(e) plus extra: sign convention of cycle indexing, whether L must divide the number of samples, whether to allow harmonics (CycleNet takes single L), dataset sampling frequency (some ETT at 15min → L=96), etc.

