tool-a71ddd1051fa080d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# DESIGN: Autocorrelation-based identification of CycleNet's optimal cycle length\n\n**Status: design only. Nothing has been executed as an experiment. The `answer` below is a specification. All numbers under \"Sandbox probes\" are rule-debugging/tooling smoke checks on throwaway synthetic series used to debug the decision rule \u2014 they are not results about CycleNet, benchmarks, or detection accuracy.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem formalization\n\nLet `z_1..z_n` be one univariate, equally-spaced channel. CycleNet is treated as a black box exposing one hyperparameter **L** (the cycle length driving a periodic/cyclic index table and a lag-L \"partial series\" `z_t \u2212 z_{t\u2212L}`). Define:\n\n- **\u03c1_z(k)** = sample autocorrelation at lag k.\n- **L\\*_corr** = the value emitted by the detection procedure (\u00a72). *This is the correlation-only object.*\n- **L\\*_err** = argmin over L of held-out forecast error for a fixed model. *This requires a model; it is the validation reference.*\n\nThe design's core deliverable is the **agreement between L\\*_corr and L\\*_err**, plus the procedure's abstention behaviour when no defensible L exists. \"Optimal\" is used in the approved split: correlation-optimal is primary, error-optimal is the validation target.\n\n## 2. The detection procedure (pipeline **D**), pre-registered\n\nEvery threshold below is fixed in advance and reported; no per-dataset tuning.\n\n**D0 Admissibility gates \u2192 abstain.** Abstain with a *named reason* if: `n < 12`; series constant (`max==min`); or `n / L < 4` for the emitted candidate (fewer than 4 seasons). Reasons: `too_short`, `constant`, `insufficient_seasons`.\n\n**D1 Representation (pre-registered switch, not \"record variants\").** Primary representation = **raw centered series**. Compute a trend z-statistic `tz` = OLS slope / its standard error. If `|tz| > 3`, switch to **centered-moving-average-detrended** with window `W = min(2\u00b7k\u0302+1, n/4)` where `k\u0302` is the current best candidate (first pass uses `W = 49` or `n/20`, whichever is smaller), and record `representation=\"detrended\"`. Secondary confirmation: an nsdiffs-style diagnostic \u2014 does lag-L differencing reduce `|\u03c1(L)|` (a unit-root-in-seasonality check); if a candidate's `\u03c1(L)` collapses under differencing at a *shorter* lag, that shorter lag wins.\n\n**D2 Significance and prominence gates.** Search band `k \u2208 [2, k_max]`, `k_max = min(400, n/3)`. A lag is a *peak* iff it is a strict local max **and** `\u03c1(k)` **and** its prominence (height above the higher of the two flanking minima) both exceed the **Bartlett band** `1.96\u00b7sqrt((1 + 2\u03a3_{j<k}\u03c1_j\u00b2)/n)`. The prominence gate is not cosmetic: in probes it was the only thing that suppressed false positives on white noise (which produced \"significant\" ACF maxima with prominence \u2248 0.004).\n\n**D3 Adjacent-lag clustering (period space) \u2014 mandatory.** Merge peaks whose lag separation is `\u2264 max(1, round(0.02k)) + 1`; the cluster representative is the lag with maximum `\u03c1` in the cluster. **Justification (probe-verified):** without this step the true peak is evicted by an off-by-one neighbour. In one probe run the raw ACF argmax on a trended period-24 series was 25, and an un-clustered harmonic test then killed lag 24 in favour of the pseudo-prime 23 and 167 instead of 168, which also corrupted downstream harmonic logic and stability counts. This step is the single most consequential rule detail found during debugging.\n\n**D4 Harmonic / near-multiple rejection, in period space.** Reject candidate `k` if any smaller cluster representative `p` satisfies `|k \u2212 p\u00b7round(k/p)| \u2264 max(1, round(0.02p))` (tolerance is *relative to the fundamental* and rounded to \u00b11 sample so fractional periods like 24.7 are handled). Exact-integer divisibility tests are insufficient: fractional periods leak energy to non-integer multiples (probe: 49, 74, 99 for a true 24.7), which exact-divisor rules do not catch.\n\n**D5 Scoring and primary decision rule (ordinal, per your guardrail 2).** Primary rule is **threshold + ordinal**, not a weighted composite:\n- **Harmonic-family energy** `E(k) = (1/M)\u00b7\u03a3_{m=1..M} max(0, \u03c1(round(mk)))`, `M = 3`, measuring whether the period recurs (a fundamental must recur, a one-off bump does not).\n- **Selection:** among candidates, take `k_best = argmax E(k)`, then emit the **smallest** candidate with `E(k) \u2265 0.90\u00b7E(k_best)` (a parsimony/smallest-sufficient-fundamental rule). This prefers 24 over 48 without a weight, and prefers 24 over 168 when weekly energy is weak \u2014 the pre-registered rule you asked to have thresholded and reported.\n- **Ranked shortlist** = all candidates sorted by `(E, prominence, \u03c1)`, emitted alongside L\\* regardless of the selection outcome.\n- A **composite** `S(k) = w\u2081z(E) + w\u2082z(prominence) + w\u2083z(spectral prominence)` is used **only to order the shortlist**, never to select. Sensitivity check: `w \u2208 {(.5,.3,.2), (.34,.33,.33), (.6,.2,.2), (.2,.6,.2), (.2,.2,.6)}`; report how often shortlist order and, if the composite were (wrongly) used to select, the emitted L change. Expectation (labeled as expectation): selection is invariant to weights for >90% of synthetic series with SNR above the noise control, precisely because selection is threshold-based.\n\n**D6 Independent confirmations (cross-checks, reported, not averaged in).**\n- **Lag-L seasonal-differencing drop** `\u0394_L = \u03c1(L) \u2212 \u03c1_L-diff(L)`. Probe-verified as a genuine discriminator: for true periods `\u0394_L` was strongly positive (\u2248 +0.5 to +1.46); for a spurious beat-frequency candidate it was **+0.26**, i.e. differencing did *not* remove the peak. Recorded; a candidate with `\u0394_L \u2264 0` is flagged low-confidence.\n- **FFT periodogram** with Welch smoothing and parabolic log-power interpolation for non-integer periods. Probe finding that shapes the design: the spectrum is *more* trend-fragile than the ACF \u2014 on the same trended series ACF still found 24 while the raw spectrum reported period 2142 (leakage into the lowest bins). Hence the spectral arm is run **only on the D1-chosen (detrended) representation** and is a cross-check, never the selector.\n- **Seasonal strength** `1 \u2212 Var(resid)/Var(deseason+resid)` from a moving-average decomposition, reported for comparability across series.\n\n**D7 Abstention and boundary warning.** Emit no L, with reason, when: no peak passes D2 (`no_prominent_significant_peak`); all peaks are explained as harmonics (`all_peaks_harmonic`); `E(k_best) < 0.15` (`energy_below_gate`). Additionally, if `\u03c1(k_max)` exceeds its band, emit `warn: acf_elevated_at_kmax` \u2014 the true L may sit outside the searched band. Probe-verified: this fired on the true-period-120 / `k_max`=40 control instead of reporting a wrong answer.\n\n**D8 Confidence interval for L\\* (guardrail 6).** Resampling must **preserve periodicity** \u2014 probe-verified that naive block bootstrap destroys it: block length = L gave stability 0.0, 2L \u2192 0.35, 4L \u2192 0.80 on the same clean period-24 series. Pre-registered: (i) moving-block bootstrap with **block length \u2265 4L**, 200 replicates \u2192 modal lag and a 90% lag interval; (ii) circular sub-period phase shifts (L\u22121 shifts, deterministic) \u2192 phase-invariance rate; (iii) rolling windows of length `n/5` scored by the *same* D5 criterion \u2192 modal agreement fraction. A second probe-verified trap: computing stability as the **raw ACF argmax per window is degenerate** \u2014 for a clean period-24 sinusoid the argmax is lag 1 in 9/9 windows (\u03c1(1)=0.945 > \u03c1(24)=0.896) while \u03c1(24) is perfectly stable, so argmax-based stability reported 0.0 for a textbook-easy series. Stability must be scored by the same restricted candidate set + criterion as the main pipeline.\n\n**D9 Emitted record per series:** `L*`, ranked shortlist with `E`/prominence/`\u03c1`, margin to runner-up, `\u0394_L`, seasonal strength, spectral period, representation flag, stability/CI, abstention reason, warnings, and a plausible band `[L_lo, L_hi]` from D8.\n\n## 3. Handoff to CycleNet (thin, model-agnostic)\n\n`L*` \u2192 CycleNet's cycle-length hyperparameter (cyclic index period and lag-L partial series). The shortlist and `[L_lo, L_hi]` define the **validation sweep window** in \u00a76 rather than being discarded. If CycleNet requires `L \u2265` forecast horizon `H` (unverified \u2014 assumption A2), clip upward to the next shortlist member \u2265 H and log the override; if it requires `L` to divide the sample or lookback length, snap to the nearest admissible multiple of the shortlist member and log it.\n\n**Per-dataset aggregation (your (b), hierarchical).** Report the **modal per-channel recommendation plus the fraction of channels agreeing**; report the full per-channel distribution, never a bare median (a median across a bimodal 24/168 channel set yields a meaningless 96). **Interpretation for CycleNet, which takes one shared L:** modal agreement \u2265 0.8 \u2192 adopt the mode, high confidence; 0.5\u20130.8 \u2192 adopt the mode but treat it as an ambiguity case in the agreement analysis; < 0.5 \u2192 the shared-L assumption itself is violated by the dataset, and that is reported as a *finding about CycleNet's interface*, not smoothed over. Expected (labeled): multivariate benchmarks split into the all-channels-daily group (Weather/ETT/Electricity-like, agreement near 1.0) and the mixed-group case where a subset of channels is weekly.\n\n## 4. L-inflation analysis (your (c), analyzed not mentioned)\n\nTension: the cyclic parameter table and the lag-L partial series both grow with L, and larger L means fewer cycles inside the lookback `N_lb`. Pre-registered cost model, reported as a table over candidate L:\n- **Parameter cost** \u2248 `L \u00b7 d_model` extra entries (e.g. d=512: L=24 \u2192 \u224812k, L=168 \u2192 \u224886k, \u22487\u00d7 the cyclic table).\n- **Estimability constraint** `N_lb / L \u2265 4` (a few cycles needed for the cyclic table to be seen enough times to be learned).\n- **Partial-series availability**: `z_{t\u2212L}` exists only for the last `N_lb \u2212 L` inputs. The illustrative hourly configuration `N_lb=96` makes **L=168 structurally unavailable** (0.57 cycles; most partial-series entries undefined), which is the sharpest statement of the tension and is reported as a hard infeasibility, not a soft cost.\n- **Rule (thresholded, reported, never silent):** prefer the fundamental L over a coarsest-common-multiple L (24 over 168) when the secondary season's family energy is `< 0.5\u00b7` the primary's; when the ratio is in `[0.5, 1]`, emit both with the ratio and route to the \u00a76 sweep. Every application of this rule is logged.\n\n## 5. Data: admissibility, synthetic ladder, illustrative grid\n\n**Dataset admissibility criteria (replaces availability-dependence):** equally spaced, no more than ~5% gaps (else resample and log it); `n \u2265 4\u00b7k_max` or explicit abstention; at least one full train/val/test split in time order; \u2265 2 channels for panel reporting; documented sampling frequency and, where known, the domain-expected season (used only for post-hoc comparison, never for selection).\n\n**Synthetic known-period control \u2014 first-class, with a difficulty ladder.** Generator `sin(2\u03c0t/P) + a_h\u00b7sin(4\u03c0t/P) + a_s\u00b7sin(2\u03c0t/P_s) + trend\u00b7(t/n) + \u03c3\u00b7\u03b5`, with P, `a_h`, `a_s`, trend, \u03c3, n controlled. Ladder: **A0** clean single season; **A1** + trend; **A2** + rising noise to the abstention gate; **A3** + harmonic distortion `a_h \u2208 {0.5,0.8,1.2}`; **A4** secondary season swept over `a_s/a_p \u2208 {0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,1.0}` with `P_s = 7P` (the fundamental-vs-CM transition, 24 vs 168); **A5** short series (`n/P \u2208 {2,3,4,6}`, probes already show ~3 seasons correctly abstains); **A6** fractional period 24.7 near the FFT resolution limit; **A7** true P outside `k_max`; **A8** near-aliasing `P \u2208 {2,2.4,3}`, where the design must abstain; **A9** regime switch mid-series (P changes) \u2014 expectation: stability metrics degrade while point detection returns the dominant half, which is a *correct* outcome to report as such. \u2265 20 seeds per cell. Ground truth P is known by construction here and only here, which is why this arm carries the detection-accuracy claims.\n\n**Illustrative evaluation grid (described abstractly per your (a); no files fetched):** hourly ~17.5k/17.4k points, 7\u201321 channels, expected daily season 24; ~10-minute hourly-scale variants, 6\u20137 channels, expected season 96; 10-minute ~52k points, 21 channels, daily-dominant; 10-minute ~52k points, 862 channels, daily-dominant with flat overnight profiles; ~3k points, 8 channels, weak daily with a strong weekend weekly effect (the *hardest* single case for L=24); 10-minute ~27k points, 6 channels, **genuinely weak seasonality** (expected to approach abstention). Named benchmarks may be mapped onto these rows for exposition; the design depends only on the properties.\n\n**Negative / failure controls (guardrail 4), each with a pre-registered expectation:** white noise \u2192 abstain (probe: fired `energy_below_gate`, E=0.031); AR(1), \u03c1=0.9, no seasonality \u2192 abstain (probe: fired `no_prominent_significant_peak`; important because AR persistence alone creates broad autocorrelation); constant/degenerate \u2192 abstain; 3-seasons-only \u2192 abstain (probe-verified); true L outside band \u2192 abstain + boundary warning (probe-verified); fractional period \u2192 nearest integer with widened CI (probe returned 25 for 24.7, i.e. rounding behaviour must be reported explicitly, not hidden); near-aliasing P \u2248 2 \u2192 abstain; and a **beat/sum control** (`a_s=1.0`), which in probes defeated a naive prominence ranking (it returned 121/215/289/383 instead of 24) and is therefore retained as a *regression test* for the clustering + harmonic-family rules.\n\n## 6. Agreement analysis: correlation-derived L vs error-optimal L\n\n**Arms, cheapest first.**\n- **Arm 0 \u2014 correlation-only (\u00a72).** No training.\n- **Arm 1 \u2014 model-free proxy curve, no training.** For each candidate L, held-out MSE of the lag-L estimator `\u1e91_{t+h} = z_{t+h\u2212L}` (cycle-L seasonal-naive) and of a one-lag regression on the lag-L value, over the same validation folds. Cost: negligible; provides an *error-shaped* curve against which L\\*_corr can be checked with zero training.\n- **Arm 2 \u2014 specified-but-unrun CycleNet (or fixed-architecture MLP surrogate) L-sweep.** Fully specified: candidate grid = shortlist \u222a {1, mode\u00b11 step, 24, 48, 96, 168} \u222a [L_lo, L_hi]; **everything else frozen** (architecture, d_model, dropout, N_lb, H, optimizer, LR schedule, epochs, early-stopping rule, splits, normalization statistics fit on train only); \u2265 3 seeds per (series, L); standard rolling-origin validation; MAE and RMSE at H \u2208 {96, 192, 336} with the two most common horizons as primary. **This arm is not executed and no outcome is claimed.**\n\n**Agreement metrics, per series then aggregated.** Primary: **exact hit** `1[L*_corr \u2208 argmin_L error(L)]` and **within-tolerance hit** `|L*_corr \u2212 L*_err| \u2264 max(1, 0.02\u00b7L)`. **Rank agreement**: Spearman \u03c1 and Kendall \u03c4 between the `E(k)` score curve and the negative-error curve over the common candidate grid (computed on the shortlist \u222a sweep grid, so ties are bounded and reported). **Regret**: `MSE(L*_corr) \u2212 min_L MSE(L)`, plus the **oracle gap** of the *cheap* arms (what you lose by using Arm 0/1 instead of Arm 2) and the **naive prior** baseline (fixed domain period: 24 hourly / 144 at 10-min). Aggregation: macro-average over series; separately, the per-dataset modal-agreement panel from \u00a73. **Stated expectations, labeled as expectations, not results:** (i) Arm 0 exact-hit \u2265 0.7 on ladder cells A0\u2013A3 and A9-with-dominant-season, collapsing toward 0 for A2/A5/A7/A8 where abstention is the correct output; (ii) Spearman \u03c1 between E-curve and \u2212error curve materially above 0 on strong-seasonality rows and near 0 on the weak-seasonality rows; (iii) regret of Arm 0 within a few percent of the L-sweep oracle on daily-dominant hourly data, but not on the weekly-confounded row; (iv) Arm 1 (seasonal-naive) agreeing with Arm 0 far more often than with Arm 2 on long-horizon settings \u2014 a *pre-registered falsification test*: if correlation-derived and seasonal-naive-optimal L agree while both disagree with CycleNet's error-optimal L, that localizes the discrepancy to CycleNet's capacity/interface rather than to the autocorrelation analysis.\n\n## 7. Feasibility and cost analysis\n\n**Detection (Arms 0\u20131): trivially cheap.** Asymptotically `O(n log n)` per series via FFT-based ACF, `O(n\u00b7k_max)` direct; plus `O(B\u00b7n log n)` for B bootstrap replicates. Measured in this sandbox with a **pure-stdlib** implementation (numpy/scipy absent, single core): full detection on a 17,420-point series \u2248 **0.39\u20130.68 s**; rolling-window stability on the same series \u2248 **0.45 s**; 3 repeats \u2248 1.85 s. Scaling to the full illustrative grid (\u2248 900 channels) is minutes single-core. **This arm is not a feasibility concern under any budget.**\n**Validation (Arm 2): the bottleneck, and flagged as such.** `#L (\u224812) \u00d7 #series (\u2248900) \u00d7 #seeds (\u22653) \u2248 32k model fits` \u2014 GPU-class, the only compute-heavy component. Because the brief fixes design-only and this environment has no GPU, no network, and no installed scientific stack, **I deliberately commit to no compute budget.** Mitigations pre-registered for whoever runs it: restrict Arm 2 to a stratified subsample (one row per frequency class + the two hard rows), a subset of channels, and `#L \u2248 6`, cutting \u2248 32k \u2192 \u2248 1k fits; or substitute the fixed-architecture MLP surrogate.\n**Tooling (guardrail e).** Design is specified against numpy/scipy/statsmodels (`acorr`, `periodogram`/`welch`, STL, `nsdiffs`); the sandbox constraint \u2014 **all scientific packages missing, Python 3.14.4, network disabled, stdlib only** \u2014 is recorded as an environment limitation, and \u00a72 was debugged under exactly that constraint, which is why it is implementable with `math`/`cmath` alone. `/tmp` is reset between commands here, so any multi-step run must be self-contained per invocation.\n\n## 8. Assumptions, limitations, and what is *not* known\n\n- **Nothing has been run.** No experiment, no benchmark evaluation, no CycleNet training. No result is reported anywhere in this design.\n- **A1 (interface, unverified).** CycleNet exposes a single integer cycle-length hyperparameter used for a cyclic index and a lag-L partial series. Not verifiable here: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no CycleNet code, paper, config or dataset descriptor exists in the image (filesystem-wide search returned zero hits), and `track.md` is only a one-line string inside `task.json`.\n- **A2 (unverified).** Whether the cyclic parameter must cover the horizon, or whether L interacts with `N_lb`. **If false**, \u00a73's clipping rule becomes a no-op, \u00a74's `N_lb/L \u2265 4` becomes advisory rather than structural, and the \"L=168 infeasible at N_lb=96\" claim in \u00a74 is withdrawn (the row is reported as feasible-but-costly instead of infeasible). The correlation analysis in \u00a72 is unaffected either way.\n- **A3.** L may be any positive integer; if admissible values are constrained (divisors of `N_lb`, table sizes), snap-to-admissible with logging.\n- **A4.** Regularly spaced series with the season \u2265 2 samples; violated series route to abstention.\n- **A5.** Univariate treatment; cross-channel information is used only for the \u00a73 panel recommendation, so any shared-L gain is attributable to the panel rule, not to leakage.\n- **Method limitations.** ACF/periodogram assume approximate second-order stationarity, so drifting periods (\u00a7A9) yield a dominant-period answer with degraded stability \u2014 reported, not hidden. Single-L emission structurally cannot represent daily+weekly jointly; \u00a74 makes that a quantified trade-off rather than pretending it is solvable. Correlation criteria reward *predictable periodicity*, not *forecast usefulness*; a strongly periodic component can be irrelevant or harmful at long horizons, which is exactly the gap \u00a76 measures rather than assumes. Thresholds 0.90/0.15/\u00b12%/4 seasons are pre-registered as stated \u2014 defensible and probe-motivated, not derived from optimality \u2014 and are swept as a sensitivity axis. D5's ordinal rule was chosen **because** the weighted composite ranked a beat frequency above the true fundamental in probing; the failure mode is thus designed out rather than averaged over.\n- **Sandbox-probe scope.** The probe findings that shaped the design (adjacent-lag eviction, trend-induced spectral leakage to period 2142, seasonal-differencing drop discriminating true vs spurious L, block length < 4L destroying periodicity, argmax-based stability being degenerate, abstention firing correctly on noise/AR(1)/short/out-of-band series) are **rule-debugging observations on my own throwaway synthetic series**, from a non-peer-reviewed stdlib implementation. They establish that the procedure is implementable and that these specific failure modes are real; they establish nothing about detection accuracy, and are not transferred to this design as evidence about any benchmark.", "evidence": "ENVIRONMENT / REPO (verified): /workspace contains only task.json (412 bytes); filesystem-wide find for *cyclenet* and track.md returned zero hits; task.json[\"documents\"][\"track.md\"] is the one line \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Python 3.14.4; numpy, scipy, pandas, statsmodels, sklearn, torch, matplotlib, pmdarima all import-MISSING; urllib to pypi raised URLError (network disabled); nproc=224; /tmp is reset between execute_check calls, so probes must be self-contained.\n\nSANDBOX PROBES (rule-debugging only, throwaway synthetic series, NOT experiment results). Progression of self-contained pure-stdlib scripts, each fixing a defect found in the previous:\n1. Cost/timing: direct ACF, n=1000/kmax=333 0.015s; n=5000 0.132s; n=20000/kmax=500 0.562s.\n2. Defect found: raw ACF argmax on a trended period-24 series returned lag 25 (off-by-one/trend), and a naive \"penalize multiples\" harmonic rule was inverted (picked 20/21/44/45 over 24).\n3. Representation/trend test: with OLS trend z=40.3, auto-switch chose MA-detrending and returned top=[24,120]; forcing the raw representation kept ACF correct at 24 but the FFT reported period 2142.49 (trend leakage into lowest frequency bins) => spectrum must run only on the detrended representation.\n4. Defect found: with a strong secondary weekly season, prominence ranking + exact-divisor harmonic test returned spurious beat frequencies 121/215/289/383 instead of 24; seasonal-differencing drop separated them (drop +0.5..+1.46 for true periods vs +0.26 for the spurious candidate).\n5. Defect found: un-clustered adjacent lags evicted true peaks (returned 23 instead of 24, 167 instead of 168; runner-ups 71/119), because pseudo-prime neighbours bypassed the near-multiple rejection; also pure noise produced garbage ranking (E/prom energy 0.037, prominence 0.004) until a prominence-vs-band gate was added.\n6. After adding D3 cluster-merge, D4 relative-tolerance near-multiple rejection, harmonic-family energy M=3, parsimony 0.90, energy gate 0.15, and fixing a lag-index off-by-one (index i <-> lag i+1) that had corrupted both the harmonic test and the stability term: final probe returned L*=24 for clean, +strong trend, weak weekly (a_s=0.3), strong weekly (a_s=1.0), harmonic 0.8, and ETTm-scale n=17420; L*=168 for the long-season case; L*=25 for fractional 24.7 (rounding behaviour explicitly observed); abstained with named reasons on white noise (energy_below_gate, E=0.031), AR(1) rho=0.9 (no_prominent_significant_peak), constant series, 3-season series, and true P=120 with k_max=40. Full detection on n=17420: 0.39-0.68s pure Python; 3 repeats 1.85s.\n7. Stability diagnostics isolated two traps: rolling-window argmax returned lag 1 in 9/9 windows for a clean period-24 sinusoid (rho(1)=0.945 > rho(24)=0.896) giving a spuriously 0.0 stability score, proving stability must use the same restricted candidate set/criterion as selection; and moving-block bootstrap block length mattered monotonically (block=L -> 0.0, 2L -> 0.35, 4L -> 0.80), so block length >= 4L is required to preserve periodicity. Circular sub-period phase shift gave 1.0 for P=24. Rolling-window stability cost at n=17420: 0.45s.\nNo CycleNet code was run; no benchmark data was accessed; no detection-accuracy or forecast-error outcome was measured.", "assumptions": ["CycleNet exposes a single integer cycle-length hyperparameter feeding a cyclic index table and a lag-L partial series (unverified in this environment; A1)", "Whether the cyclic parameter must cover the forecast horizon, or L interacts with the lookback window, is unverified; the design states how \u00a73/\u00a74 change if false (A2)", "L may be any positive integer; admissibility snapping is logged if constraints exist (A3)", "Series are equally spaced with the target season >= 2 samples; violations abstain (A4)", "Named benchmarks are an illustrative grid described by properties only; nothing is fetched", "Nothing has been executed as an experiment; all probe numbers are rule-debugging observations on throwaway synthetic data, not findings about accuracy or benchmarks", "No compute budget is committed for the L-sweep arm; it is specified-but-unrun"], "uncertainties": ["The true CycleNet interface and its L constraints remain unverified because no CycleNet source or paper is present in the image", "Thresholds 0.90 parsimony, 0.15 energy gate, \u00b12% near-multiple tolerance, 4-season minimum are pre-registered judgment calls, swept as a sensitivity axis rather than derived", "Whether correlation-optimal L tracks error-optimal L at long horizons is the open empirical question, not a design input", "Per-channel disagreement in panel datasets with one shared L is expected but unquantified until run", "GPU cost of the specified L-sweep is estimated by count (~32k fits full, ~1k reduced) with no measured per-fit time, since no training is possible here", "Fractional-period rounding (24.7 -> 25) and near-aliasing behaviour need confirmation with an FFT-resolution-aware CI rather than integer rounding"], "stage": "result"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-647253e9-0b7a-42e0-be94-1f490160d2e3", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"answer": "# DESIGN: Autocorrelation-based identification of CycleNet's optimal cycle length\n\n**Status: design only. Nothing has been executed as an experiment. The `answer` below is a specification. All numbers under \"Sandbox probes\" are rule-debugging/tooling smoke checks on throwaway synthetic series used to debug the decision rule \u2014 they are not results about CycleNet, benchmarks, or detection accuracy.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem formalization\n\nLet `z_1..z_n` be one univariate, equally-spaced channel. CycleNet is treated as a black box exposing one hyperparameter **L** (the cycle length driving a periodic/cyclic index table and a lag-L \"partial series\" `z_t \u2212 z_{t\u2212L}`). Define:\n\n- **\u03c1_z(k)** = sample autocorrelation at lag k.\n- **L\\*_corr** = the value emitted by the detection procedure (\u00a72). *This is the correlation-only object.*\n- **L\\*_err** = argmin over L of held-out forecast error for a fixed model. *This requires a model; it is the validation reference.*\n\nThe design's core deliverable is the **agreement between L\\*_corr and L\\*_err**, plus the procedure's abstention behaviour when no defensible L exists. \"Optimal\" is used in the approved split: correlation-optimal is primary, error-optimal is the validation target.\n\n## 2. The detection procedure (pipeline **D**), pre-registered\n\nEvery threshold below is fixed in advance and reported; no per-dataset tuning.\n\n**D0 Admissibility gates \u2192 abstain.** Abstain with a *named reason* if: `n < 12`; series constant (`max==min`); or `n / L < 4` for the emitted candidate (fewer than 4 seasons). Reasons: `too_short`, `constant`, `insufficient_seasons`.\n\n**D1 Representation (pre-registered switch, not \"record variants\").** Primary representation = **raw centered series**. Compute a trend z-statistic `tz` = OLS slope / its standard error. If `|tz| > 3`, switch to **centered-moving-average-detrended** with window `W = min(2\u00b7k\u0302+1, n/4)` where `k\u0302` is the current best candidate (first pass uses `W = 49` or `n/20`, whichever is smaller), and record `representation=\"detrended\"`. Secondary confirmation: an nsdiffs-style diagnostic \u2014 does lag-L differencing reduce `|\u03c1(L)|` (a unit-root-in-seasonality check); if a candidate's `\u03c1(L)` collapses under differencing at a *shorter* lag, that shorter lag wins.\n\n**D2 Significance and prominence gates.** Search band `k \u2208 [2, k_max]`, `k_max = min(400, n/3)`. A lag is a *peak* iff it is a strict local max **and** `\u03c1(k)` **and** its prominence (height above the higher of the two flanking minima) both exceed the **Bartlett band** `1.96\u00b7sqrt((1 + 2\u03a3_{j<k}\u03c1_j\u00b2)/n)`. The prominence gate is not cosmetic: in probes it was the only thing that suppressed false positives on white noise (which produced \"significant\" ACF maxima with prominence \u2248 0.004).\n\n**D3 Adjacent-lag clustering (period space) \u2014 mandatory.** Merge peaks whose lag separation is `\u2264 max(1, round(0.02k)) + 1`; the cluster representative is the lag with maximum `\u03c1` in the cluster. **Justification (probe-verified):** without this step the true peak is evicted by an off-by-one neighbour. In one probe run the raw ACF argmax on a trended period-24 series was 25, and an un-clustered harmonic test then killed lag 24 in favour of the pseudo-prime 23 and 167 instead of 168, which also corrupted downstream harmonic logic and stability counts. This step is the single most consequential rule detail found during debugging.\n\n**D4 Harmonic / near-multiple rejection, in period space.** Reject candidate `k` if any smaller cluster representative `p` satisfies `|k \u2212 p\u00b7round(k/p)| \u2264 max(1, round(0.02p))` (tolerance is *relative to the fundamental* and rounded to \u00b11 sample so fractional periods like 24.7 are handled). Exact-integer divisibility tests are insufficient: fractional periods leak energy to non-integer multiples (probe: 49, 74, 99 for a true 24.7), which exact-divisor rules do not catch.\n\n**D5 Scoring and primary decision rule (ordinal, per your guardrail 2).** Primary rule is **threshold + ordinal**, not a weighted composite:\n- **Harmonic-family energy** `E(k) = (1/M)\u00b7\u03a3_{m=1..M} max(0, \u03c1(round(mk)))`, `M = 3`, measuring whether the period recurs (a fundamental must recur, a one-off bump does not).\n- **Selection:** among candidates, take `k_best = argmax E(k)`, then emit the **smallest** candidate with `E(k) \u2265 0.90\u00b7E(k_best)` (a parsimony/smallest-sufficient-fundamental rule). This prefers 24 over 48 without a weight, and prefers 24 over 168 when weekly energy is weak \u2014 the pre-registered rule you asked to have thresholded and reported.\n- **Ranked shortlist** = all candidates sorted by `(E, prominence, \u03c1)`, emitted alongside L\\* regardless of the selection outcome.\n- A **composite** `S(k) = w\u2081z(E) + w\u2082z(prominence) + w\u2083z(spectral prominence)` is used **only to order the shortlist**, never to select. Sensitivity check: `w \u2208 {(.5,.3,.2), (.34,.33,.33), (.6,.2,.2), (.2,.6,.2), (.2,.2,.6)}`; report how often shortlist order and, if the composite were (wrongly) used to select, the emitted L change. Expectation (labeled as expectation): selection is invariant to weights for >90% of synthetic series with SNR above the noise control, precisely because selection is threshold-based.\n\n**D6 Independent confirmations (cross-checks, reported, not averaged in).**\n- **Lag-L seasonal-differencing drop** `\u0394_L = \u03c1(L) \u2212 \u03c1_L-diff(L)`. Probe-verified as a genuine discriminator: for true periods `\u0394_L` was strongly positive (\u2248 +0.5 to +1.46); for a spurious beat-frequency candidate it was **+0.26**, i.e. differencing did *not* remove the peak. Recorded; a candidate with `\u0394_L \u2264 0` is flagged low-confidence.\n- **FFT periodogram** with Welch smoothing and parabolic log-power interpolation for non-integer periods. Probe finding that shapes the design: the spectrum is *more* trend-fragile than the ACF \u2014 on the same trended series ACF still found 24 while the raw spectrum reported period 2142 (leakage into the lowest bins). Hence the spectral arm is run **only on the D1-chosen (detrended) representation** and is a cross-check, never the selector.\n- **Seasonal strength** `1 \u2212 Var(resid)/Var(deseason+resid)` from a moving-average decomposition, reported for comparability across series.\n\n**D7 Abstention and boundary warning.** Emit no L, with reason, when: no peak passes D2 (`no_prominent_significant_peak`); all peaks are explained as harmonics (`all_peaks_harmonic`); `E(k_best) < 0.15` (`energy_below_gate`). Additionally, if `\u03c1(k_max)` exceeds its band, emit `warn: acf_elevated_at_kmax` \u2014 the true L may sit outside the searched band. Probe-verified: this fired on the true-period-120 / `k_max`=40 control instead of reporting a wrong answer.\n\n**D8 Confidence interval for L\\* (guardrail 6).** Resampling must **preserve periodicity** \u2014 probe-verified that naive block bootstrap destroys it: block length = L gave stability 0.0, 2L \u2192 0.35, 4L \u2192 0.80 on the same clean period-24 series. Pre-registered: (i) moving-block bootstrap with **block length \u2265 4L**, 200 replicates \u2192 modal lag and a 90% lag interval; (ii) circular sub-period phase shifts (L\u22121 shifts, deterministic) \u2192 phase-invariance rate; (iii) rolling windows of length `n/5` scored by the *same* D5 criterion \u2192 modal agreement fraction. A second probe-verified trap: computing stability as the **raw ACF argmax per window is degenerate** \u2014 for a clean period-24 sinusoid the argmax is lag 1 in 9/9 windows (\u03c1(1)=0.945 > \u03c1(24)=0.896) while \u03c1(24) is perfectly stable, so argmax-based stability reported 0.0 for a textbook-easy series. Stability must be scored by the same restricted candidate set + criterion as the main pipeline.\n\n**D9 Emitted record per series:** `L*`, ranked shortlist with `E`/prominence/`\u03c1`, margin to runner-up, `\u0394_L`, seasonal strength, spectral period, representation flag, stability/CI, abstention reason, warnings, and a plausible band `[L_lo, L_hi]` from D8.\n\n## 3. Handoff to CycleNet (thin, model-agnostic)\n\n`L*` \u2192 CycleNet's cycle-length hyperparameter (cyclic index period and lag-L partial series). The shortlist and `[L_lo, L_hi]` define the **validation sweep window** in \u00a76 rather than being discarded. If CycleNet requires `L \u2265` forecast horizon `H` (unverified \u2014 assumption A2), clip upward to the next shortlist member \u2265 H and log the override; if it requires `L` to divide the sample or lookback length, snap to the nearest admissible multiple of the shortlist member and log it.\n\n**Per-dataset aggregation (your (b), hierarchical).** Report the **modal per-channel recommendation plus the fraction of channels agreeing**; report the full per-channel distribution, never a bare median (a median across a bimodal 24/168 channel set yields a meaningless 96). **Interpretation for CycleNet, which takes one shared L:** modal agreement \u2265 0.8 \u2192 adopt the mode, high confidence; 0.5\u20130.8 \u2192 adopt the mode but treat it as an ambiguity case in the agreement analysis; < 0.5 \u2192 the shared-L assumption itself is violated by the dataset, and that is reported as a *finding about CycleNet's interface*, not smoothed over. Expected (labeled): multivariate benchmarks split into the all-channels-daily group (Weather/ETT/Electricity-like, agreement near 1.0) and the mixed-group case where a subset of channels is weekly.\n\n## 4. L-inflation analysis (your (c), analyzed not mentioned)\n\nTension: the cyclic parameter table and the lag-L partial series both grow with L, and larger L means fewer cycles inside the lookback `N_lb`. Pre-registered cost model, reported as a table over candidate L:\n- **Parameter cost** \u2248 `L \u00b7 d_model` extra entries (e.g. d=512: L=24 \u2192 \u224812k, L=168 \u2192 \u224886k, \u22487\u00d7 the cyclic table).\n- **Estimability constraint** `N_lb / L \u2265 4` (a few cycles needed for the cyclic table to be seen enough times to be learned).\n- **Partial-series availability**: `z_{t\u2212L}` exists only for the last `N_lb \u2212 L` inputs. The illustrative hourly configuration `N_lb=96` makes **L=168 structurally unavailable** (0.57 cycles; most partial-series entries undefined), which is the sharpest statement of the tension and is reported as a hard infeasibility, not a soft cost.\n- **Rule (thresholded, reported, never silent):** prefer the fundamental L over a coarsest-common-multiple L (24 over 168) when the secondary season's family energy is `< 0.5\u00b7` the primary's; when the ratio is in `[0.5, 1]`, emit both with the ratio and route to the \u00a76 sweep. Every application of this rule is logged.\n\n## 5. Data: admissibility, synthetic ladder, illustrative grid\n\n**Dataset admissibility criteria (replaces availability-dependence):** equally spaced, no more than ~5% gaps (else resample and log it); `n \u2265 4\u00b7k_max` or explicit abstention; at least one full train/val/test split in time order; \u2265 2 channels for panel reporting; documented sampling frequency and, where known, the domain-expected season (used only for post-hoc comparison, never for selection).\n\n**Synthetic known-period control \u2014 first-class, with a difficulty ladder.** Generator `sin(2\u03c0t/P) + a_h\u00b7sin(4\u03c0t/P) + a_s\u00b7sin(2\u03c0t/P_s) + trend\u00b7(t/n) + \u03c3\u00b7\u03b5`, with P, `a_h`, `a_s`, trend, \u03c3, n controlled. Ladder: **A0** clean single season; **A1** + trend; **A2** + rising noise to the abstention gate; **A3** + harmonic distortion `a_h \u2208 {0.5,0.8,1.2}`; **A4** secondary season swept over `a_s/a_p \u2208 {0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,1.0}` with `P_s = 7P` (the fundamental-vs-CM transition, 24 vs 168); **A5** short series (`n/P \u2208 {2,3,4,6}`, probes already show ~3 seasons correctly abstains); **A6** fractional period 24.7 near the FFT resolution limit; **A7** true P outside `k_max`; **A8** near-aliasing `P \u2208 {2,2.4,3}`, where the design must abstain; **A9** regime switch mid-series (P changes) \u2014 expectation: stability metrics degrade while point detection returns the dominant half, which is a *correct* outcome to report as such. \u2265 20 seeds per cell. Ground truth P is known by construction here and only here, which is why this arm carries the detection-accuracy claims.\n\n**Illustrative evaluation grid (described abstractly per your (a); no files fetched):** hourly ~17.5k/17.4k points, 7\u201321 channels, expected daily season 24; ~10-minute hourly-scale variants, 6\u20137 channels, expected season 96; 10-minute ~52k points, 21 channels, daily-dominant; 10-minute ~52k points, 862 channels, daily-dominant with flat overnight profiles; ~3k points, 8 channels, weak daily with a strong weekend weekly effect (the *hardest* single case for L=24); 10-minute ~27k points, 6 channels, **genuinely weak seasonality** (expected to approach abstention). Named benchmarks may be mapped onto these rows for exposition; the design depends only on the properties.\n\n**Negative / failure controls (guardrail 4), each with a pre-registered expectation:** white noise \u2192 abstain (probe: fired `energy_below_gate`, E=0.031); AR(1), \u03c1=0.9, no seasonality \u2192 abstain (probe: fired `no_prominent_significant_peak`; important because AR persistence alone creates broad autocorrelation); constant/degenerate \u2192 abstain; 3-seasons-only \u2192 abstain (probe-verified); true L outside band \u2192 abstain + boundary warning (probe-verified); fractional period \u2192 nearest integer with widened CI (probe returned 25 for 24.7, i.e. rounding behaviour must be reported explicitly, not hidden); near-aliasing P \u2248 2 \u2192 abstain; and a **beat/sum control** (`a_s=1.0`), which in probes defeated a naive prominence ranking (it returned 121/215/289/383 instead of 24) and is therefore retained as a *regression test* for the clustering + harmonic-family rules.\n\n## 6. Agreement analysis: correlation-derived L vs error-optimal L\n\n**Arms, cheapest first.**\n- **Arm 0 \u2014 correlation-only (\u00a72).** No training.\n- **Arm 1 \u2014 model-free proxy curve, no training.** For each candidate L, held-out MSE of the lag-L estimator `\u1e91_{t+h} = z_{t+h\u2212L}` (cycle-L seasonal-naive) and of a one-lag regression on the lag-L value, over the same validation folds. Cost: negligible; provides an *error-shaped* curve against which L\\*_corr can be checked with zero training.\n- **Arm 2 \u2014 specified-but-unrun CycleNet (or fixed-architecture MLP surrogate) L-sweep.** Fully specified: candidate grid = shortlist \u222a {1, mode\u00b11 step, 24, 48, 96, 168} \u222a [L_lo, L_hi]; **everything else frozen** (architecture, d_model, dropout, N_lb, H, optimizer, LR schedule, epochs, early-stopping rule, splits, normalization statistics fit on train only); \u2265 3 seeds per (series, L); standard rolling-origin validation; MAE and RMSE at H \u2208 {96, 192, 336} with the two most common horizons as primary. **This arm is not executed and no outcome is claimed.**\n\n**Agreement metrics, per series then aggregated.** Primary: **exact hit** `1[L*_corr \u2208 argmin_L error(L)]` and **within-tolerance hit** `|L*_corr \u2212 L*_err| \u2264 max(1, 0.02\u00b7L)`. **Rank agreement**: Spearman \u03c1 and Kendall \u03c4 between the `E(k)` score curve and the negative-error curve over the common candidate grid (computed on the shortlist \u222a sweep grid, so ties are bounded and reported). **Regret**: `MSE(L*_corr) \u2212 min_L MSE(L)`, plus the **oracle gap** of the *cheap* arms (what you lose by using Arm 0/1 instead of Arm 2) and the **naive prior** baseline (fixed domain period: 24 hourly / 144 at 10-min). Aggregation: macro-average over series; separately, the per-dataset modal-agreement panel from \u00a73. **Stated expectations, labeled as expectations, not results:** (i) Arm 0 exact-hit \u2265 0.7 on ladder cells A0\u2013A3 and A9-with-dominant-season, collapsing toward 0 for A2/A5/A7/A8 where abstention is the correct output; (ii) Spearman \u03c1 between E-curve and \u2212error curve materially above 0 on strong-seasonality rows and near 0 on the weak-seasonality rows; (iii) regret of Arm 0 within a few percent of the L-sweep oracle on daily-dominant hourly data, but not on the weekly-confounded row; (iv) Arm 1 (seasonal-naive) agreeing with Arm 0 far more often than with Arm 2 on long-horizon settings \u2014 a *pre-registered falsification test*: if correlation-derived and seasonal-naive-optimal L agree while both disagree with CycleNet's error-optimal L, that localizes the discrepancy to CycleNet's capacity/interface rather than to the autocorrelation analysis.\n\n## 7. Feasibility and cost analysis\n\n**Detection (Arms 0\u20131): trivially cheap.** Asymptotically `O(n log n)` per series via FFT-based ACF, `O(n\u00b7k_max)` direct; plus `O(B\u00b7n log n)` for B bootstrap replicates. Measured in this sandbox with a **pure-stdlib** implementation (numpy/scipy absent, single core): full detection on a 17,420-point series \u2248 **0.39\u20130.68 s**; rolling-window stability on the same series \u2248 **0.45 s**; 3 repeats \u2248 1.85 s. Scaling to the full illustrative grid (\u2248 900 channels) is minutes single-core. **This arm is not a feasibility concern under any budget.**\n**Validation (Arm 2): the bottleneck, and flagged as such.** `#L (\u224812) \u00d7 #series (\u2248900) \u00d7 #seeds (\u22653) \u2248 32k model fits` \u2014 GPU-class, the only compute-heavy component. Because the brief fixes design-only and this environment has no GPU, no network, and no installed scientific stack, **I deliberately commit to no compute budget.** Mitigations pre-registered for whoever runs it: restrict Arm 2 to a stratified subsample (one row per frequency class + the two hard rows), a subset of channels, and `#L \u2248 6`, cutting \u2248 32k \u2192 \u2248 1k fits; or substitute the fixed-architecture MLP surrogate.\n**Tooling (guardrail e).** Design is specified against numpy/scipy/statsmodels (`acorr`, `periodogram`/`welch`, STL, `nsdiffs`); the sandbox constraint \u2014 **all scientific packages missing, Python 3.14.4, network disabled, stdlib only** \u2014 is recorded as an environment limitation, and \u00a72 was debugged under exactly that constraint, which is why it is implementable with `math`/`cmath` alone. `/tmp` is reset between commands here, so any multi-step run must be self-contained per invocation.\n\n## 8. Assumptions, limitations, and what is *not* known\n\n- **Nothing has been run.** No experiment, no benchmark evaluation, no CycleNet training. No result is reported anywhere in this design.\n- **A1 (interface, unverified).** CycleNet exposes a single integer cycle-length hyperparameter used for a cyclic index and a lag-L partial series. Not verifiable here: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no CycleNet code, paper, config or dataset descriptor exists in the image (filesystem-wide search returned zero hits), and `track.md` is only a one-line string inside `task.json`.\n- **A2 (unverified).** Whether the cyclic parameter must cover the horizon, or whether L interacts with `N_lb`. **If false**, \u00a73's clipping rule becomes a no-op, \u00a74's `N_lb/L \u2265 4` becomes advisory rather than structural, and the \"L=168 infeasible at N_lb=96\" claim in \u00a74 is withdrawn (the row is reported as feasible-but-costly instead of infeasible). The correlation analysis in \u00a72 is unaffected either way.\n- **A3.** L may be any positive integer; if admissible values are constrained (divisors of `N_lb`, table sizes), snap-to-admissible with logging.\n- **A4.** Regularly spaced series with the season \u2265 2 samples; violated series route to abstention.\n- **A5.** Univariate treatment; cross-channel information is used only for the \u00a73 panel recommendation, so any shared-L gain is attributable to the panel rule, not to leakage.\n- **Method limitations.** ACF/periodogram assume approximate second-order stationarity, so drifting periods (\u00a7A9) yield a dominant-period answer with degraded stability \u2014 reported, not hidden. Single-L emission structurally cannot represent daily+weekly jointly; \u00a74 makes that a quantified trade-off rather than pretending it is solvable. Correlation criteria reward *predictable periodicity*, not *forecast usefulness*; a strongly periodic component can be irrelevant or harmful at long horizons, which is exactly the gap \u00a76 measures rather than assumes. Thresholds 0.90/0.15/\u00b12%/4 seasons are pre-registered as stated \u2014 defensible and probe-motivated, not derived from optimality \u2014 and are swept as a sensitivity axis. D5's ordinal rule was chosen **because** the weighted composite ranked a beat frequency above the true fundamental in probing; the failure mode is thus designed out rather than averaged over.\n- **Sandbox-probe scope.** The probe findings that shaped the design (adjacent-lag eviction, trend-induced spectral leakage to period 2142, seasonal-differencing drop discriminating true vs spurious L, block length < 4L destroying periodicity, argmax-based stability being degenerate, abstention firing correctly on noise/AR(1)/short/out-of-band series) are **rule-debugging observations on my own throwaway synthetic series**, from a non-peer-reviewed stdlib implementation. They establish that the procedure is implementable and that these specific failure modes are real; they establish nothing about detection accuracy, and are not transferred to this design as evidence about any benchmark.", "evidence": "ENVIRONMENT / REPO (verified): /workspace contains only task.json (412 bytes); filesystem-wide find for *cyclenet* and track.md returned zero hits; task.json[\"documents\"][\"track.md\"] is the one line \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Python 3.14.4; numpy, scipy, pandas, statsmodels, sklearn, torch, matplotlib, pmdarima all import-MISSING; urllib to pypi raised URLError (network disabled); nproc=224; /tmp is reset between execute_check calls, so probes must be self-contained.\n\nSANDBOX PROBES (rule-debugging only, throwaway synthetic series, NOT experiment results). Progression of self-contained pure-stdlib scripts, each fixing a defect found in the previous:\n1. Cost/timing: direct ACF, n=1000/kmax=333 0.015s; n=5000 0.132s; n=20000/kmax=500 0.562s.\n2. Defect found: raw ACF argmax on a trended period-24 series returned lag 25 (off-by-one/trend), and a naive \"penalize multiples\" harmonic rule was inverted (picked 20/21/44/45 over 24).\n3. Representation/trend test: with OLS trend z=40.3, auto-switch chose MA-detrending and returned top=[24,120]; forcing the raw representation kept ACF correct at 24 but the FFT reported period 2142.49 (trend leakage into lowest frequency bins) => spectrum must run only on the detrended representation.\n4. Defect found: with a strong secondary weekly season, prominence ranking + exact-divisor harmonic test returned spurious beat frequencies 121/215/289/383 instead of 24; seasonal-differencing drop separated them (drop +0.5..+1.46 for true periods vs +0.26 for the spurious candidate).\n5. Defect found: un-clustered adjacent lags evicted true peaks (returned 23 instead of 24, 167 instead of 168; runner-ups 71/119), because pseudo-prime neighbours bypassed the near-multiple rejection; also pure noise produced garbage ranking (E/prom energy 0.037, prominence 0.004) until a prominence-vs-band gate was added.\n6. After adding D3 cluster-merge, D4 relative-tolerance near-multiple rejection, harmonic-family energy M=3, parsimony 0.90, energy gate 0.15, and fixing a lag-index off-by-one (index i <-> lag i+1) that had corrupted both the harmonic test and the stability term: final probe returned L*=24 for clean, +strong trend, weak weekly (a_s=0.3), strong weekly (a_s=1.0), harmonic 0.8, and ETTm-scale n=17420; L*=168 for the long-season case; L*=25 for fractional 24.7 (rounding behaviour explicitly observed); abstained with named reasons on white noise (energy_below_gate, E=0.031), AR(1) rho=0.9 (no_prominent_significant_peak), constant series, 3-season series, and true P=120 with k_max=40. Full detection on n=17420: 0.39-0.68s pure Python; 3 repeats 1.85s.\n7. Stability diagnostics isolated two traps: rolling-window argmax returned lag 1 in 9/9 windows for a clean period-24 sinusoid (rho(1)=0.945 > rho(24)=0.896) giving a spuriously 0.0 stability score, proving stability must use the same restricted candidate set/criterion as selection; and moving-block bootstrap block length mattered monotonically (block=L -> 0.0, 2L -> 0.35, 4L -> 0.80), so block length >= 4L is required to preserve periodicity. Circular sub-period phase shift gave 1.0 for P=24. Rolling-window stability cost at n=17420: 0.45s.\nNo CycleNet code was run; no benchmark data was accessed; no detection-accuracy or forecast-error outcome was measured.", "assumptions": ["CycleNet exposes a single integer cycle-length hyperparameter feeding a cyclic index table and a lag-L partial series (unverified in this environment; A1)", "Whether the cyclic parameter must cover the forecast horizon, or L interacts with the lookback window, is unverified; the design states how \u00a73/\u00a74 change if false (A2)", "L may be any positive integer; admissibility snapping is logged if constraints exist (A3)", "Series are equally spaced with the target season >= 2 samples; violations abstain (A4)", "Named benchmarks are an illustrative grid described by properties only; nothing is fetched", "Nothing has been executed as an experiment; all probe numbers are rule-debugging observations on throwaway synthetic data, not findings about accuracy or benchmarks", "No compute budget is committed for the L-sweep arm; it is specified-but-unrun"], "uncertainties": ["The true CycleNet interface and its L constraints remain unverified because no CycleNet source or paper is present in the image", "Thresholds 0.90 parsimony, 0.15 energy gate, \u00b12% near-multiple tolerance, 4-season minimum are pre-registered judgment calls, swept as a sensitivity axis rather than derived", "Whether correlation-optimal L tracks error-optimal L at long horizons is the open empirical question, not a design input", "Per-channel disagreement in panel datasets with one shared L is expected but unquantified until run", "GPU cost of the specified L-sweep is estimated by count (~32k fits full, ~1k reduced) with no measured per-fit time, since no training is possible here", "Fractional-period rounding (24.7 -> 25) and near-aliasing behaviour need confirmation with an FFT-resolution-aware CI rather than integer rounding"], "stage": "result"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
